WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.015 --> 00:00:06.890
MORGAN MCLEAN: Hi, and
welcome to our session

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:09.980
Ops 203, OpenTelemetry
and observability

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.467
at Shopify, Splunk, and Google.

00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:13.550
My name is Morgan McClean.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:14.900
I'm a product manager at Google.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:17.180
I primarily focus on
OpenTelemetry as well as

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.650
a number of our ops
management products,

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.708
including Cloud Monitoring
and Cloud Logging.

00:00:22.708 --> 00:00:24.500
STEVE FLANDERS: And
hi, I'm Steve Flanders.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.330
I'm a director of
engineering at Splunk

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:29.340
working on our observability
getting data in strategy.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.146
I'm an OpenTelemetry
collector approver.

00:00:32.146 --> 00:00:34.021
MORGAN MCLEAN: We have
a packed agenda today,

00:00:34.021 --> 00:00:35.979
and we're going to have
interesting information

00:00:35.979 --> 00:00:38.280
for those who are familiar
of OpenTelemetry and those

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.208
who are not.

00:00:39.208 --> 00:00:41.250
We'll begin with an
introduction to the project--

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.370
what OpenTelemetry is and
what the project includes.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.110
Then we'll move on
to OpenTelemetry

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:49.560
and how it fits into the
broader field of observability.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.420
After that, we will
show getting started

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:54.327
guides and examples of how
you can use OpenTelemetry.

00:00:54.327 --> 00:00:56.160
And finally, we'll have
stories of companies

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.860
that make use of OpenTelemetry
in their production services

00:00:58.860 --> 00:00:59.880
today.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.130
OpenTelemetry has a number
of different components,

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:05.069
and I'm going to start from
the most basic ones all

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:06.630
the way to the core
implementations

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.180
that customers are using
today in production.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.190
To start with, we have
the specification.

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.040
The specification is, in many
ways, the core of the project.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.320
It defines the different
signals and different data types

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:19.200
that you can use to
capture telemetry

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.440
from your application.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.560
For example, there's
a specification

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.087
for metrics that defines
what a metric is,

00:01:25.087 --> 00:01:27.420
what a metric can capture,
and how you interact with it.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.640
Similarly, there are
specifications for traces,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.560
resources, and logs.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.140
Keep in mind that the log
specification is relatively new

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.870
compared to the others,
so it's still incubating.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.140
Next we have a data model.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.430
The data model describes
how actual APIs

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.680
and how your code will
interact with this data,

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.340
and how a metric, or trace,
or log, or other component

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.270
is actually described
to a computer system.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.040
Next we have API services.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.650
These are the different ways
that your code will interact

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:59.160
with the data model in order
to capture correct telemetry

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.480
from your application.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.478
These API services
include instrumentation,

00:02:02.478 --> 00:02:04.770
which allow you to go and
create a metric for something

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:08.400
like an RPC call, or to
define what a trace will look

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.740
like when you're capturing
it from a certain source;

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.810
configuration, which
allow you to interact

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:15.660
with other parts of the
OpenTelemetry APIs and SDKs,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.240
like sampling for example;
and finally, there's

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.800
an API for exporters.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.380
Exporters are how we send
data from an OpenTelemetry

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.570
SDK or other
OpenTelemetry resource

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.970
to other back end components.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.730
These back end components might
be the OpenTelemetry collector,

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:30.500
which is an agent.

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:31.960
We'll get to what
that is in a moment.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.085
They can do that through
the standard OpenTelemetry

00:02:34.085 --> 00:02:36.820
interchange protocol, or
you can use an exporter

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:40.350
to send data directly to the
back end like Google Cloud

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.590
Ops or Splunk.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.050
Finally, we also defined
semantic conventions.

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.270
These semantic
conventions are not

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:50.310
so much rules for the
machine, but rules

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:53.340
for people who are defining
instrumentation or capturing

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.542
data from their applications.

00:02:55.542 --> 00:02:57.000
An example of a
semantic convention

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.722
would be how to create a metric
for HTTP latency or HTTP QPS.

00:03:02.722 --> 00:03:04.680
It would include the tags
that you're generally

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.010
expected to add there,
and the metric names

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:08.010
that you would generate.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.220
The same is true for tracing.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.750
The next level up
in OpenTelemetry

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.910
is the actual APIs and SDKs.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.982
We have one API and
SDK per language,

00:03:15.982 --> 00:03:17.690
so there are no
competing implementations

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.330
and all effort is driven behind
that single implementation

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:21.980
for language.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:25.130
OpenTelemetry, as mentioned,
has the standard SDKs.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.862
Because we've APIs, you can also
go ahead an SDK of your own.

00:03:27.862 --> 00:03:29.570
As far as I know I
don't think anyone is,

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.330
but it's totally available
for vendors or anyone else

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.860
to go to find their own SDK.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:39.305
And the APIs allow you to go
use the API surfaces that I

00:03:39.305 --> 00:03:41.680
described before to actually
go and write instrumentation

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:44.760
or to interact with
the SDKs themselves.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.770
The SDKs contain
logic for things

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:49.952
like sampling, or creating a
trace, or creating a metric,

00:03:49.952 --> 00:03:51.660
and so we generally
recommend that people

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:52.800
use the standard SDKs.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.530
That seems to be what most
the community is doing.

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:56.700
Next we have the collector.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.650
This is an optional component.

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.290
The collector runs as an agent.

00:04:00.290 --> 00:04:04.950
So whereas the SDKs you would
link them to your application

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.440
to go capture traces and
metrics, the collector

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.760
you would instead run
on the operating system.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.270
The collector generally can't
capture traces and metrics

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:13.680
directly from your application.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.690
Those have to be done
through the SDKs.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:17.820
But it can be used as
a processor or proxy

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.620
that you send that
data to before it gets

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.940
exported to your
final destination,

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.420
and it makes things like
configuration a little easier,

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.540
and it makes just a generally
cleaner, easier export path

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.760
to send data to your
back end of choice.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:32.010
The collector can also
capture system metrics.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.490
So metrics from places where
you can't use the API and SDK.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.730
Think of memory consumption
or disc consumption.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:39.810
You can do this for
Windows and Linux.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.790
And the collector can
be deployed as an agent

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.050
on a particular
operating system--

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.790
in that case you also get the
system metrics if you want

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:45.570
them--

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:47.700
or it can be
deployed as a service

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.620
so that you can access it and
send data over the network.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:52.560
The collector also
contains receivers

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:55.710
for metrics from Prometheus
and traces captured

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:58.128
from Zipkin or Jaeger clients.

00:04:58.128 --> 00:04:59.670
There's also some
new components that

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.110
have been added to
OpenTelemetry fairly recently.

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:05.220
These are auto instrumentation
agents for Java, Python,

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.460
and JavaScript, and we're adding
some other languages soon.

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.590
These will take some of
the functionality you would

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:14.122
normally get in the SDK, like
capturing traces from an app,

00:05:14.122 --> 00:05:16.080
and they are able to
perform that functionality

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.320
without having to link to your
application and rebuild it.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.530
Now, they're not as
generally useful as the SDKs.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:25.710
The benefit of the SDKs is you
can go to find a custom metric,

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.430
or make changes to
trace annotations,

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:31.080
or use the any of the APIs
natively that you want to.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.330
You can't do that with the
auto instrumentation agent.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:34.788
But if you have an
application that

00:05:34.788 --> 00:05:36.363
is difficult to
recompile or rebuild,

00:05:36.363 --> 00:05:38.280
or one that you don't
have the source code to,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.480
the auto instrumentation
agent is a great choice.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:44.347
OpenTelemetry entered
beta in March.

00:05:44.347 --> 00:05:45.930
This is a big effort
by the community,

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.140
and we're incredibly proud of
the accomplishments we've had.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.780
And this is why you see some
firms and some organizations

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:54.900
already using some OpenTelemetry
components in production

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.190
systems.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:58.140
Specifically, the
March beta included

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:03.450
the Java, Python, JavaScript,
and Go SDKs, as well as

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:05.180
the Erlang SDK.

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:07.750
And I think the .net SDK is
about to hit beta or already

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:08.910
just has.

00:06:08.910 --> 00:06:11.160
The beta also included
the collector.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.950
The beta was very focused
on tracing functionality

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:16.080
and also included
metrics functionality.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.382
We're targeting a GA
later this autumn.

00:06:20.382 --> 00:06:22.590
Prior to the GA we'll have
several release candidates

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.907
coming up that will
be pre-GA versions.

00:06:25.907 --> 00:06:27.990
And eventually once we're
very satisfied with one,

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.640
we'll declare that as
the final GA version.

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:32.850
Expect those release
candidates to start rolling out

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.490
later this summer and
the GA to take place then

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.345
later this year.

00:06:37.345 --> 00:06:38.720
STEVE FLANDERS:
OK, so let's talk

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:41.390
about observability,
specifically in the context

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:42.770
of OpenTelemetry.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:44.823
As Morgan mentioned,
there's a specification

00:06:44.823 --> 00:06:46.490
which is basically
the foundational part

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:48.290
of the OpenTelemetry project.

00:06:48.290 --> 00:06:50.640
This specification can
be data source specific.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.360
So for example, there are
specifications for both traces

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:56.142
and metrics, and inside
each of those specifications

00:06:56.142 --> 00:06:58.100
is some rich information
about how you actually

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.140
define the information--
the telemetry

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:01.700
that you're trying to admit.

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:04.520
In the case of traces
this includes the spans

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:07.850
and different attributes that
are attached to those spans.

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:10.050
In the case of metrics, you
have labels and the ways

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.830
you actually account for
the different metrics

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:14.503
that you care about
in your environment.

00:07:14.503 --> 00:07:15.920
You can actually
drill into these.

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:18.030
They're all on the
GitHub repository

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:22.070
if you're interested in learning
more information about them.

00:07:22.070 --> 00:07:23.850
MORGAN MCLEAN: Next
we have resources.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:26.350
So as the slide describes,
every OpenTelemetry signal

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:27.623
is tied to a resource.

00:07:27.623 --> 00:07:29.540
And if you think about
this, this makes sense.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:32.000
If you're capturing metrics
from your application or traces

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.490
from your application, they're
not particularly useful

00:07:34.490 --> 00:07:36.020
unless you know
where each metric

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.540
or where each span of a
trace actually came from.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:40.460
There's different levels
to these resources.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:43.130
You can go as low level as
the actual information that

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:46.100
describes the actual machine
that a metric or trace came

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:48.650
from all the way up to
higher level concepts

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:51.230
like services, or
Kubernetes containers,

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:53.810
or deployments from which
you're capturing this data.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.310
STEVE FLANDERS:
Semantic conventions

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:57.230
are also extremely powerful.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:59.270
The idea is, how do
I actually define

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:00.680
this thing that I'm calling?

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.230
Like for example, if
I have a REST endpoint

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:05.810
or I'm calling a database,
if there was a way

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:08.480
to just normalize that
information so it didn't matter

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:10.670
which vendor or back end
I'm sending my data to,

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:12.800
I could still query it
out in a unique way,

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.240
that'd be extremely powerful.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:16.910
And that's exactly what
semantic conventions try to do.

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:19.880
By offering a standard way
of actually instrumenting it,

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:22.170
you can then leverage any
back end of your choice

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.670
in order to query the data out
and get the insights that you

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:25.310
care about.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.240
True observability.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.570
Now in the case of tracing,
context propagation

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.580
is probably one of the
most fundamental pieces.

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:35.020
It's basically how you
glue everything together.

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.020
So when you have your
service to service calls,

00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:39.965
you're actually passing
context between those calls.

00:08:39.965 --> 00:08:41.340
You actually see
a representation

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:43.120
of this on the right hand side.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.570
This is actually from
the Jaeger documentation.

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:47.550
Basically, you call
into a service,

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:49.440
and you generate context.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.180
You then pass that
context as you

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.160
make other calls to
downstream services

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:54.420
within your environment.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.670
That context can also be
shared within the application

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.570
for functional level calls.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:00.900
But this context
propagation concept

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:03.330
actually goes beyond
distributed tracing.

00:09:03.330 --> 00:09:05.580
For example, I probably
want to share metadata

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:07.480
between my different
data sources,

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.530
and I can do that through
the correlation context

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:12.360
specification information.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:14.160
In addition, I need a
way to actually share

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:17.840
that between my APIs and
SDKs, and the propagators

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:20.398
data's is how we do that in
the OpenTelemetry project.

00:09:20.398 --> 00:09:22.440
There are links to all
this information if you're

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:24.695
really interested in
learning the specifics of it,

00:09:24.695 --> 00:09:26.070
but this is really
the magic that

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:28.710
glues all the information
together and makes it extremely

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:31.260
useful when you want to actually
query out and get insights

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:32.320
from them.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.320
So let's talk a little
bit about getting started

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:35.745
with the OpenTelemetry project.

00:09:35.745 --> 00:09:37.620
The easiest way to do
that is to really start

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:39.580
talking about the architecture.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:41.610
So you can see here
we have a host.

00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:44.220
There's an application running
on it, and maybe we have one

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:47.310
or even multiple back ends
we want to send our data to.

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:50.423
How do we get started
with OpenTelemetry?

00:09:50.423 --> 00:09:52.340
Well, first we're going
to go ahead and deploy

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:54.680
the OpenTelemetry
collector as an agent.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.450
The reason why we do
this is kind of twofold.

00:09:57.450 --> 00:10:00.140
One is it can go ahead and
grab the system metrics for you

00:10:00.140 --> 00:10:02.180
automatically and send
that to the back end

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:03.950
or back ends of your choice.

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:06.830
Secondarily, if I actually
leverage the OpenTelemetry

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:10.160
collector as an agent with the
OpenTelemetry client libraries,

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:13.040
then it's automatically
configured for me thanks

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:15.530
to the use of the OTLP
protocol that is built

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:18.490
into the OpenTelemetry project.

00:10:18.490 --> 00:10:21.750
So if I deploy the OpenTelemetry
client library and an agent

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:24.420
is already running there,
the traffic will be received,

00:10:24.420 --> 00:10:27.600
both metrics and
traces, eventually logs,

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.640
and all that data can
then be sent to one

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.890
or more back ends based
on configuration rules

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:34.020
that I write in the
collector itself.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:37.260
This provides flexibility
and also offloads

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:40.200
some of the responsibility
from the application itself--

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:43.390
things like buffering, and
retry, encryption, compression.

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:45.840
All that can actually be
offloaded from the application,

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:48.540
which would consume resources
and actually be handled

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.750
in a single language-- in
the case of the collector,

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.190
it is Go--

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:58.008
one way and be handled
across my entire fleet.

00:10:58.008 --> 00:10:59.800
Another deployment
architecture is actually

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:02.470
to have the collector running
as a standalone service.

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:05.440
That would be typically in the
same data center or region.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:08.560
I would deploy one or more
of these collector instances,

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:12.430
and usually it's the agent that
will forward to the collector.

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:14.230
Now, the idea here
is maybe I want

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:16.108
to limit the number
of egress points

00:11:16.108 --> 00:11:17.150
I have in my environment.

00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:19.400
So I don't want all the
agents to talk to the internet

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:21.340
to send to my
vendors' back ends.

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:23.500
Maybe I want to
consolidate the API keys.

00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:26.260
Maybe I want bigger
buffer or retry queues,

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:29.080
and so I can offload this
to a set of collectors

00:11:29.080 --> 00:11:32.150
that I can scale up
or out as needed.

00:11:32.150 --> 00:11:34.250
There are different
use cases for this.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:36.130
Another one would
be around tracing.

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:38.140
Maybe I want to do
tail based sampling.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:40.330
That typically doesn't
follow an agent model.

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:42.250
You typically need
standalone collectors

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:44.830
in order to achieve that, and
the OpenTelemetry collector

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:46.600
is actually a single
binary that can

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:49.680
be deployed as either an agent
or as a standalone service

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:52.840
and is very configurable
depending on your use cases.

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:55.630
So next I want to talk
about auto instrumentation.

00:11:55.630 --> 00:11:58.000
Because if you've never got
started with instrumentation

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:00.873
before, it may seem like a
lot to actually get going.

00:12:00.873 --> 00:12:02.290
And really,
OpenTelemetry's trying

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:04.780
to make this be as
easy as possible.

00:12:04.780 --> 00:12:07.220
And then later on, you can
get to manual instrumentation

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:09.970
if you want to add your own
custom metric or tracing

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:12.220
information, your own
metadata, and enhance what's

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:13.863
available out of the box.

00:12:13.863 --> 00:12:15.280
The idea with auto
instrumentation

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:17.740
is it's basically
runtime parameter changes

00:12:17.740 --> 00:12:20.240
to your application,
not code changes.

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:22.490
So it's very easy
to get started.

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.460
Here's an example of
a Java application.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:27.460
Let's say I have a Java
call to a JAR-- the JAR

00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:29.680
that I've written that
runs my application.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:33.280
All I need to do is go ahead and
define a Java agent parameter

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:37.035
and point to the OpenTelemetry
Java agent all JAR file.

00:12:37.035 --> 00:12:38.410
In addition here,
I've gone ahead

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:40.035
and set an environmental
variable which

00:12:40.035 --> 00:12:41.210
will set the service name.

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:42.913
So I know what this
service actually is

00:12:42.913 --> 00:12:44.830
and how it will be
represented in the back end

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:46.780
that I'm forwarding
that traffic to.

00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:47.290
That's it.

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:49.520
I then just fire
up my application,

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.580
and I can go ahead and get
rich trace data automatically

00:12:52.580 --> 00:12:54.340
for any supported library.

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:57.050
And there is broad
support for this today.

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:58.728
Here's an example,
in the case of Java,

00:12:58.728 --> 00:13:00.520
of all the different
libraries and versions

00:13:00.520 --> 00:13:01.510
that are supported.

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:03.640
Now, this slide is probably
already out of date

00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:06.390
because new ones are being
added every single day, so

00:13:06.390 --> 00:13:08.890
go ahead and take a look at
this and feel free to contribute

00:13:08.890 --> 00:13:09.850
additional ones.

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:12.130
But it provides broad
support and really can

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.800
get you started with
distributed tracing information

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:16.600
with little
development involvement

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:18.010
in order to get you started.

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:19.180
Now with that, I'd
like to show you

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:20.930
a demo of how you can
actually get started

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:23.770
with both the OpenTelemetry
collector and with Java

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:25.730
auto instrumentation.

00:13:25.730 --> 00:13:29.732
OK, let's go ahead and fire
up the demo application.

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:35.990
While it starts up here, I'll
explain to you what's going on.

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:38.870
First, we're going to be
leveraging the Spring Pet

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:42.260
Clinic micro services
example application.

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:45.440
It's all based on
Java, and it's actually

00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:47.450
multiple different
microservices that

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:49.100
communicate with one another.

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:51.050
As you can see,
they have a picture

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:53.480
on the repository
on what it looks

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:56.150
like from an
architectural perspective,

00:13:56.150 --> 00:13:58.250
and the applications
are actually

00:13:58.250 --> 00:14:00.410
not instrumented by default.

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:04.700
So our goal here is going to be
to add OpenTelemetry Java auto

00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:08.150
instrumentation to the
Java microservices,

00:14:08.150 --> 00:14:12.140
and to have them report to an
OpenTelemetry collector which

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:16.890
can then send to the back end
or back ends of your choice.

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:18.710
So what I've done
is I've actually

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:21.080
modified this repository.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:23.990
I've added a
collector.yaml which

00:14:23.990 --> 00:14:26.900
we'll go ahead and define
how we want the OpenTelemetry

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:29.000
collector to be configured.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.490
In this case, I'm
basically just accepting

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:34.850
over the OTLP
protocol and defining

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:37.070
an endpoint where I can
actually send traffic

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:39.440
to a Zipkin server.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:42.890
And this only needs
to be defined once.

00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:47.630
In addition, basically how that
Spring Pet Clinic microservices

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:50.300
example app works is through
a Docker compose file.

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:54.050
So each of the microservices is
a different Docker container.

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:57.020
I've gone ahead and made
the following modification.

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:01.090
Basically, I've enabled the Java
agent runtime parameter, which

00:15:01.090 --> 00:15:03.650
you pass first right
after the Java commands,

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:05.690
and I'm pointing it to
the OpenTelemetry Java

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:07.790
agent all JAR file.

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:10.490
And then I am defining
a service name

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:12.690
via an environmental variable.

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:15.300
So in this case, we have
the discovery service,

00:15:15.300 --> 00:15:18.140
and I've repeated that for
every single microservice that's

00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:19.460
in the Docker compose.

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:24.040
You'll see customers,
and visits, and vets.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:27.220
Finally, I have defined
an OpenTelemetry collector

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:29.770
that actually gets
spun up as well

00:15:29.770 --> 00:15:31.390
so that these
microservices can all

00:15:31.390 --> 00:15:34.420
report to a locally running
OpenTelemetry collector

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:38.620
that uses the collector.yaml
file that I just showed.

00:15:38.620 --> 00:15:41.980
For each of the microservices
we have a Docker file,

00:15:41.980 --> 00:15:45.520
and in that Docker file we will
go ahead and get the JAR file

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:49.450
from the OpenTelemetry project
so that we can embed it

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:51.970
at runtime as a
parameter and have

00:15:51.970 --> 00:15:55.940
it do byte code manipulation
of our application.

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:57.670
So that's about it.

00:15:57.670 --> 00:16:00.490
And then what ends up happening
is this microservices app

00:16:00.490 --> 00:16:03.700
gets fired up and
starts reporting traffic

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:05.110
to an OpenTelemetry collector.

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:07.150
You can actually
see here log lines

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:10.610
of different spans already
being recorded into the system.

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:12.430
So just by firing it
up, we haven't even

00:16:12.430 --> 00:16:13.720
accessed the UI yet.

00:16:13.720 --> 00:16:16.840
You can see that data is
being generated and being sent

00:16:16.840 --> 00:16:19.250
to the back ends of my choice.

00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:22.840
So I will go ahead and sort
out the pet clinic application.

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:25.240
You can see it's here.

00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:28.630
We can go ahead and look
at, let's say, any owners

00:16:28.630 --> 00:16:31.180
that are in the system,
any veterinarians that

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:32.290
are in the system.

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:34.540
We can go ahead and add
additional information,

00:16:34.540 --> 00:16:35.082
as well.

00:16:35.082 --> 00:16:36.790
You can actually
interact with the system

00:16:36.790 --> 00:16:38.450
in multiple different ways.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:41.200
And the net result here
is, in the case of Zipkin,

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:45.520
and we can go ahead and
look for any traces that

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:46.970
are coming into the system.

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:48.650
We can do this by service name.

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:50.530
So for example,
maybe I'll go ahead

00:16:50.530 --> 00:16:54.940
and go take a look
at Discovery service

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:58.390
and see that there are actually
traces coming into the system.

00:16:58.390 --> 00:17:00.340
And we can actually
drill into these

00:17:00.340 --> 00:17:02.890
and see a variety
of different calls

00:17:02.890 --> 00:17:05.615
are being made and
associated metadata.

00:17:05.615 --> 00:17:07.990
We can actually see this is
coming from the OpenTelemetry

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:09.829
project, as well.

00:17:09.829 --> 00:17:11.740
Now, I've actually
modified the collector

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:15.230
to also send to the
signal effects back end.

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:18.000
So here's another example where,
again, the data is being sent

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:19.130
to two different back ends.

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:21.790
So I can actually compare,
or I can send different datas

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:23.079
to different back ends.

00:17:23.079 --> 00:17:25.900
Really, OpenTelemetry is
looking to be vendor agnostic

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:28.630
and provide a generic way
of handling instrumentation

00:17:28.630 --> 00:17:30.850
for you so you can do
it once and not have

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:32.750
to worry about it afterwards.

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:34.240
So exact same data here.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:36.400
As you can see, you
get a visual topology.

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.950
This basically represents what
we saw in the architecture

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:41.770
diagram of the GitHub repo.

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:45.340
And as you can see, I made no
Java changes in order to get

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:48.400
this instrumentation and
actually get some rich insights

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:52.270
both from a visual topology
perspective as well as my RED

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:54.530
metrics-- request,
error, and duration--

00:17:54.530 --> 00:17:57.160
and any other metadata
that's associated with it.

00:17:57.160 --> 00:17:58.660
So it's a very quick
demo on how you

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:01.750
can get started with
OpenTelemetry, both the auto

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:04.000
instrumentation
and the collector.

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:06.490
And without making any
code modifications, only

00:18:06.490 --> 00:18:09.130
runtime parameters, we're able
to get some rich telemetry

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:11.410
data out of our application.

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:13.160
MORGAN MCLEAN: Thanks
for the demo, Steve.

00:18:13.160 --> 00:18:14.758
As I mentioned at
the beginning, there

00:18:14.758 --> 00:18:17.300
are already companies and groups
that are using OpenTelemetry

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:19.250
in production services today.

00:18:19.250 --> 00:18:20.750
Google is one of them.

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:22.430
Starting back in
January, we actually

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:24.380
deployed the
OpenTelemetry collector

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:26.930
into instances of
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:29.730
versions 1.16 and above.

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:31.620
The OpenTelemetry
collector since then

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:35.300
has formed the backbone of the
metrics pipeline inside of GKE

00:18:35.300 --> 00:18:37.610
and replaced a number
of deprecated components

00:18:37.610 --> 00:18:38.750
like Heapster.

00:18:38.750 --> 00:18:40.790
Similarly, we'll be
using OpenTelemetry

00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:43.430
for CloudOps customers,
particularly Cloud Monitoring

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:47.170
Cloud Trace, to replace the
existing OpenCensus based SDKs

00:18:47.170 --> 00:18:49.820
that they use to capture
application metrics, custom

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:52.150
metrics, and distributed traces.

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:54.760
Next, we're going to be using
open symmetry inside of Google

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:55.720
Compute Engine.

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:58.000
Have a new windows
system monitoring agent

00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:00.190
that's actually based on
the OpenTelemetry collector

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:03.300
using a lot of the monitoring
system metrics capabilities

00:19:03.300 --> 00:19:05.020
I talked about during
the introduction.

00:19:05.020 --> 00:19:06.940
Beyond this, we'll be
using the collector

00:19:06.940 --> 00:19:11.410
to replace our Linux monitoring
agent probably later this year

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:12.942
or early next
year, and that will

00:19:12.942 --> 00:19:14.650
bring in the same
capabilities that we're

00:19:14.650 --> 00:19:16.360
bringing to Windows right now.

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:18.920
At that point, I think
effectively all of our metrics

00:19:18.920 --> 00:19:21.460
and tracing stack will be
moved over to OpenTelemetry,

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:24.160
at least when it comes to
capturing data from customers'

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:25.251
applications.

00:19:30.507 --> 00:19:33.090
STEVE FLANDERS: At Splunk, we're
also leveraging OpenTelemetry

00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:34.253
extensively.

00:19:34.253 --> 00:19:35.670
We've basically
had this broken up

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:37.380
into two different
categories of work.

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.250
Internally we want
to actually consume

00:19:39.250 --> 00:19:41.560
and leverage OpenTelemetry
as much as possible.

00:19:41.560 --> 00:19:44.370
So in the case of
SignalFX and Omnition,

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:47.070
all of the infrastructure today
is leveraging the OpenTelemetry

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:49.530
collector in order to
collect both metrics

00:19:49.530 --> 00:19:50.970
and trace information.

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:52.830
We're actually actively
underway moving

00:19:52.830 --> 00:19:55.830
to OpenTelemetry client
libraries as well in order

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:59.190
to get rich insights and
move to an open source vendor

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:02.370
agnostic and open standard way
of gathering telemetry data

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:04.410
from our own environments.

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:06.960
Externally, we are active
contributors to the project.

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:09.000
We're actually maintainers
of the collector

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:12.058
as well as the Java
auto instrumentation,

00:20:12.058 --> 00:20:14.100
and we're working very
closely with the community

00:20:14.100 --> 00:20:16.950
to donate some of the SignalFX
open source components

00:20:16.950 --> 00:20:20.370
around the smart agent, gateway,
and other auto instrumentation

00:20:20.370 --> 00:20:22.930
semantic conventions
we've been working on.

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:25.650
Finally, we're about to get
kicked off with the .net auto

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:26.730
instrumentation sig.

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:28.688
So if you're interested
in that and really want

00:20:28.688 --> 00:20:31.170
to get involved, we'd
love to have your help.

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:33.990
All right, next up, let's hear
from Francis about how Shopify

00:20:33.990 --> 00:20:37.340
is leveraging OpenTelemetry.

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.710
FRANCIS BOGSANYL: Shopify is
partway through a migration

00:20:39.710 --> 00:20:42.500
from a custom trace collection
infrastructure to one based

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:44.840
heavily around OpenTelemetry.

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:47.090
In addition, Shopify is
one of the maintainers

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:49.910
of the OpenTelemetry
Ruby client library.

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:53.160
At present, our architecture
looks like this.

00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:54.980
We have a large number
of trace services

00:20:54.980 --> 00:20:58.675
written in several languages and
instrumented in different ways.

00:20:58.675 --> 00:21:00.300
Some of these are
externally developed,

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:02.510
such as [INAUDIBLE] and NGINX.

00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:04.910
Some use our custom
instrumentation and export

00:21:04.910 --> 00:21:06.650
in its custom format.

00:21:06.650 --> 00:21:09.525
Some export in Jaeger's
format, some in OpenCensus,

00:21:09.525 --> 00:21:10.150
some in Zipkin.

00:21:13.940 --> 00:21:17.520
The OpenTelemetry collector is
the core of our architecture.

00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:19.490
We add a couple of
components to it,

00:21:19.490 --> 00:21:21.650
in particular a receiver
for our custom trace

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:24.280
format and our custom exporter.

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:27.850
Our use cases for the collector
include PII redaction,

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:30.790
down sampling for cost
control, adding tags required

00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:34.570
by vendors, renaming spans
to control cardinality,

00:21:34.570 --> 00:21:36.250
and fixing
instrumentation flaws.

00:21:39.240 --> 00:21:41.970
Here's an example of PII
reduction in the collector,

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:44.250
focusing mainly on
database queries attached

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:46.740
to spans by the
instrumentation but also

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:49.070
with app-specific redaction.

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:52.500
At the top, we redact any
database statement tags

00:21:52.500 --> 00:21:54.570
unless the span also
explicitly indicates

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:56.500
that no PII is present.

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:58.580
While we're down, we
identified that PII

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:01.620
can leak into the job
args tag for this service,

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:03.620
so we explicitly
redact those tags.

00:22:06.077 --> 00:22:08.410
STEVE FLANDERS: So you just
heard from a cloud provider,

00:22:08.410 --> 00:22:11.260
Google, a vendor,
Splunk, and an end user,

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:13.960
Shopify, about different
experiences leveraging

00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:15.280
OpenTelemetry.

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:18.430
As you can see, there is broad
support and broad interest

00:22:18.430 --> 00:22:19.640
in this topic.

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:23.290
In fact, OpenTelemetry has
the second most contributions

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:26.620
in all of CNCF behind
only Kubernetes.

00:22:26.620 --> 00:22:28.150
That's pretty exciting.

00:22:28.150 --> 00:22:30.430
And we would love to
have you come join us.

00:22:30.430 --> 00:22:32.943
You can get involved in
the conversation on Gitter,

00:22:32.943 --> 00:22:35.110
you can come join a special
interest group depending

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:37.200
on which area you
care most about,

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:40.720
and there are tons of great
issues both with help wanted

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:43.517
and good first issue
labels to get you started.

00:22:43.517 --> 00:22:45.470
Please come join the community.

00:22:45.470 --> 00:22:47.042
Please come join the discussion.

00:22:47.042 --> 00:22:49.000
As Morgan mentioned,
we're just getting started

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:50.572
with the logging specification.

00:22:50.572 --> 00:22:52.030
So if you're
interested in logging,

00:22:52.030 --> 00:22:54.430
now's a great time to get
involved in that, as well.

00:22:54.430 --> 00:22:56.830
We would love to have you
be part of the solution

00:22:56.830 --> 00:23:00.270
to this important problem
for the industry as a whole.

00:23:00.270 --> 00:23:02.602
And with that, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:23:02.602 --> 00:23:04.060
We really appreciate
it and we hope

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:07.170
to see you in the
OpenTelemetry community soon.

